Subject: the object payload is a view, and every cast macro is a call

A zeo heap VALUE is a handle whose first two words are a real struct RBasic.
That much is MRI's layout, so RBASIC_CLASS, RB_FL_TEST_RAW, RB_BUILTIN_TYPE,
RB_TYPE_P, OBJ_FROZEN and RTYPEDDATA_P need no patch at all, and an extension
that sets FL_USER3 writes the word that owns it.

Behind those two words there is no struct RString and no struct RArray. This
series answers that with ONE rule, applied to all eight payload structs:

  A payload struct carries UPSTREAM'S LAYOUT. `X(obj)` is a call, not a cast,
  and answers a view the runtime owns. The view ALIASES real storage wherever
  zeo owns that storage as C-shaped memory, and is REFILLED from the object
  on every reach wherever it does not. A field zeo has no answer for is zero,
  and the accessor named for it raises.

So every struct body stays exactly upstream's, and the delta is eight macro
definitions plus the header that declares the entries. That is deliberate:
the previous series DELETED the eight struct bodies, which put 700 lines of
upstream context under the patch and made every ruby bump a merge risk.

  - `ruby/internal/zeo.h` is new: it declares the eight `rb_zeo_r*` entries
    and states the rule once.
  - RData and RTypedData ALIAS. The cell lives inside the object, so
    `RTYPEDDATA(o)->data = p` writes the object's own slot and cannot go
    stale. DATA_PTR, RTYPEDDATA_DATA, _TYPE, _GET_DATA, _EMBEDDED_P and the
    offsetof static assert are therefore all upstream code, unpatched. This
    is the line date's `d_lite_marshal_load` needs.
  - RString, RArray, RObject, RMatch, RRegexp and RFile REFILL. zeo answers
    the non-embedded shape for String and Object (RSTRING_NOEMBED and
    ROBJECT_HEAP set on the object) and never sets RARRAY_EMBED_FLAG, so
    upstream's own arms take the heap path and RSTRING_PTR/LEN/END,
    RARRAY_LEN/AREF/CONST_PTR/PTR, ROBJECT_FIELDS, RREGEXP_SRC and
    RMATCH_REGS all stay upstream code too.
  - Three macros change beyond the cast, each for a stated reason.
    RARRAY_ASET calls in, because the pointer RARRAY_PTR_USE hands out is a
    projection and a store through it would stay there. RMATCH_EXT walks off
    the VIEW rather than off the VALUE, since upstream's `m` IS the struct
    and zeo's is a handle. RREGEXP_PTR raises: the compiled pattern belongs
    to zeo's regexp engine, which may recompile it, and rb_reg_prepare_re
    refuses for the same reason.

Not patched, deliberately: rstruct.h, rhash.h and rclass.h were already all
function calls; rbignum.h reads only flags; RARRAY_PTR_USE keeps upstream's
rb_ary_ptr_use_start/_end pair.

